  {
    "sha": "9fa01e8b102329162a0bbe4ac4701f5db3ba828e",
    "node_id": "C_kwDOVoKkgNoAKDlmYTAxZThiMTAyMzI5MTYyYTBiYmU0YWM0NzAxZjVkYjNiYTgyOGU",
    "commit": {
      "author": {
        "name": "tbrasselle",
        "email": "tbrasselle@gmail.com",
        "date": "2026-11-22T14:10:00Z"
      },
      "committer": {
        "name": "tbrasselle",
        "email": "tbrasselle@gmail.com",
        "date": "2026-11-22T14:10:08Z"
      },
      "message": "fix(multiaxis): close the Rotary Offset Zigzag levels with a ring that skips the seam\n\nThe #3129 wall-finish rings were suppressed whole on the rotary wrap,\nbecause a closed ring there would run down both cover ends - the seam`s\ntwo copies at one azimuth - and cut it twice. That left every wall\nperpendicular to the rows (a D-flat`s edge walls) with cusps between row\nends.\n\nThe ring now drops only the seam edges: a boundary loop that spans a\nwhole period keeps one period of boundary per leftover arc, closed on\nthe work (each arc ends on the seam copy of its start azimuth), while\nthe rows cut the seam columns. A loop that wraps no azimuth closes as\nthe planar ring always did. The no-seam path is unchanged.\n\nPinned by the new rotary_zigzag_rings suite: the wrapped ring cuts no\nazimuth twice, and a D-flat`s edge walls finish within the wall-reach\nbound (a 0.071 mm cusp without the ring, none with it).\n\nFixes #3168",
